---
// src/pages/about.astro
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const elsewhere = [
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/morayo0x', external: true },
  { label: 'X', href: 'https://x.com/morayo0x', external: true },
  { label: 'RSS', href: '/rss.xml', external: false },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Morayo — computer science at the University of Lagos, by way of physics. AI curriculum, machine learning from first principles, and the places where formal systems meet institutions."
>
  <div class="container-reading about">
    <header class="about-head">
      <h1 class="about-title">About</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="lead">
      <p>
        I'm Morayo. I study computer science at the University of Lagos, though most of how I think
        was set earlier, by physics — by the habit of asking what a thing is actually doing before
        asking how to compute with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I work as assistant to a professor who directs an AI research lab, and alongside the
        director of the university's AI Unipod. A good deal of that work is instructional design:
        deciding what a person needs to understand, in what order, and what can honestly be left
        out.
      </p>
      <p>
        The thread running through all of it — the research, the teaching, the writing here — is
        what happens where formal systems meet the institutions that have to carry them. A proof
        holds or it does not. A curriculum has to survive a room, a timetable, and a lecturer who
        did not choose it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2 class="label">Currently</h2>
      <dl class="items">
        <div class="item">
          <dt class="item-name">A National Diploma in AI</dt>
          <dd class="item-body">
            Train-the-trainer material for polytechnic lecturers, built against the national
            technical education standards. Mathematics, programming, Python, and a laboratory course
            — four syllabuses that have to work in the hands of people teaching them for the first
            time.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="item">
          <dt class="item-name">Technical training for the lab</dt>
          <dd class="item-body">
            A course running from mathematical foundations through autograd, evaluation, and
            attention-based architectures. Every topic split in two: the theory session, then the
            lab. Engineering alone teaches people to reach for a library before they know what it is
            for.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="item">
          <dt class="item-name">An upskilling programme at scale</dt>
          <dd class="item-body">
            Helping organise AI training for penultimate and final-year students across the
            university's science faculties. Most of the difficulty is not technical.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="item">
          <dt class="item-name">An ethics workshop grounded in Ubuntu</dt>
          <dd class="item-body">
            Built to be run rather than presented. It resists slides, which I take as a sign it is
            about the right thing.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2 class="label">Learning</h2>
      <p class="block-body">
        A slow pass through machine learning from first principles — attention and what the three
        projections are actually for, backpropagation derived rather than recited, embeddings,
        tokenization down to the bytes, and the formal learnability results that say what cannot be
        got from examples at all.
      </p>
      <p class="block-body">
        Separately, a self-directed data project I am deliberately doing the hard way: no answers
        handed over, only better questions. The point is to build the instinct, and an instinct is
        not transferable by explanation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2 class="label">Also</h2>
      <p class="block-body">
        Philosophy, seriously rather than decoratively: Ubuntu ethics, virtue ethics,
        existentialism. And the same shape wherever it turns up — Lagrangians, Pareto frontiers,
        constrained optimisation. You have something to maximise, you cannot have all of it, and the
        interesting question is never the optimum but what the boundary is made of and who drew it.
      </p>
      <p class="block-body">
        Arch and Hyprland, because I would rather know what broke. Obsidian, because I would rather
        find it again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2 class="label">This site</h2>
      <p class="block-body">
        The home page is a plot rather than a list. Every piece takes a position: how far it sits
        from the human scale toward the cosmic one, and how far it has been carried from a hunch
        toward something checked. Threads are drawn through the pieces that keep asking the same
        question, and a thread is allowed to say it is unfinished.
      </p>
      <p class="block-body">
        A list by date would say that the only relation between two pieces is which came first. That
        is the one relation here that carries no information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2 class="label">Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul class="links" role="list">
        {
          elsewhere.map(({ label, href, external }) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={href}
                class="link"
                target={external ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={external ? 'noopener' : undefined}
              >
                {label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="block-body note">Lagos, Nigeria.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-top: 3.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .about-head {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .about-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.125rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lead {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .lead p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .lead p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .block {
    margin-bottom: 2.75rem;
    max-width: 66ch;
  }

  .block .label {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .block-body {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .block-body:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ── Currently ── */
  .items {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .item {
    padding: 0.875rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .item:first-child {
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  .item-name {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
  }

  .item-body {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Elsewhere ── */
  .links {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .link {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    padding-bottom: 2px;
    transition:
      color var(--transition-fast),
      border-color var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .link:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-bottom-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .note {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-faint);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
